TI  - The Influence of GLP-1 Receptor Agonists on the Outcomes and Tolerability of Colorectal Cancer Therapies.
AU  - Bs Vaishnavi Attili
AU  - Kwon Steve
JO  - Current oncology reports
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1007/s11912-026-01862-8
AB  - Glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RAs) are increasingly being prescribed for metabolic diseases. At times, patients taking GLP-1RAs are also undergoing cancer treatment and surgeries, but their potential interactions with cancer therapies are not well delineated. The purpose of this narrative review is to synthesize the limited pre-clinical, translational, and the clinical evidence available, examining the biological rationale and mechanisms for GLP-1RA-cancer therapy interactions. Oncology research on GLP-1RAs has primarily focused on cancer incidence and survival, yielding generally reassuring safety data and potential outcome improvements. While emerging evidence and biological studies suggest GLP-1RAs could influence treatment tolerance and toxicity, the current clinical data remains limited, observational, and poorly integrated. However, there has been decent mechanistic research that suggests biologically plausible interactions of GLP1-RAs and cancer therapies, specifically through the AMPK and mTOR pathways. The available clinical evidence suggests that GLP-1 receptor agonist use is associated withimproved survival outcomes and reduced treatment-related complications among patients with colorectalcancer receiving chemotherapy, immunotherapy, or surgery. However, these findings are mostlyobservational, and conflicting preclinical evidence highlights the need for prospective studies to clarifythe underlying mechanisms, establish causality, and determine the clinical implications of theseinteractions.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42983002/
