TI  - Trends in use of glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists in obese patients with endometrial cancer.
AU  - Chang Heidi H
AU  - Hsieh Tina Yi Jin
AU  - Esselen Katharine M
AU  - Hacker Michele R
AU  - Nicholson Kaitlin
AU  - Dottino Joseph A
JO  - Gynecologic oncology
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.ygyno.2026.10.030
AB  - Most endometrial cancers (EC) are obesity-related; yet, there is little known about utilization of glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist (GLP-1RAs), which are effective weight loss medications, in EC. This is a retrospective cohort study using TriNetX US Collaborative Network, a de-identified, longitudinal electronic health record dataset. Female patients were identified from 1/1/2018-4/15/2024 with BMI ≥30 and grouped by presence or absence of EC diagnosis. Patients were followed for two years or up to 4/15/26, whichever came first. These two groups were 1:1 propensity score matched for demographics, BMI, and comorbidities. Patients with prior bariatric surgery or GLP-1RA use were excluded. The primary outcome was GLP-1RA use. Subgroup analysis was conducted among patients with BMI ≥40. From 2018 to 2024, GLP-1RAs use in both cohorts increased. However, utilization was consistently lower among patients with EC. In 2018, GLP-1RA use was 1.0% among patients with EC vs. 1.2% among those without EC (RR 0.82, CI 0.60-1.13). By 2024, use increased to 7.8% and 10.6%, respectively (RR 0.73, CI 0.61-0.87). Similar findings were observed among patients with a BMI ≥40, with significantly faster uptake of GLP-1RAs among women without EC. Patients with EC were less likely to receive GLP-1RAs than matched patients without EC despite obesity being a major risk factor for EC. These findings highlight a potential gap in obesity management among EC survivors and support further investigation of the safety and efficacy of GLP-1RAs in this population.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42966334/
