ID  - 42947552
TI  - Normal-BMI MASLD is Associated with Increased Mortality, Cardiovascular Events, Cancer and Lower GLP-1 Use.
AU  - Casimiro Isabel
AU  - Agawa Aoi
AU  - Tacata Rachel Haide Naraval
AU  - Lim Eunjung
AU  - Kuwada Scott K
JO  - The Journal of clinical endocrinology and metabolism
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1210/clinem/dgag439
AB  - Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) can occur in individuals who are not overweight or have obesity (normal-BMI MASLD), yet disease burden and management remain poorly characterized. We compared long-term outcomes and incretin-based therapy use between normal-BMI MASLD (nbMASLD) and overweight/obesity-associated MASLD (obMASLD) across racial and ethnic groups. We conducted a retrospective, observational study using the TriNetX United States Network. Adults diagnosed with MASLD or metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH) from 2005-2025 were classified using ethnicity-informed normal-BMI thresholds: <23 kg/m2 for Asian, <27 kg/m2 for Native Hawaiian and Pacific Islander, and <25 kg/m2 for White/others. Propensity-score matching balanced demographic and clinical covariates. Outcomes were assessed using Kaplan-Meier analyses and Cox proportional hazards models with obMASLD as the reference group. Among 619,171 eligible patients, 51,940 matched pairs were identified. Nb-BMI MASLD was associated with higher all-cause mortality across grouped ethnic groups, with survival of 77.49% versus 83.93% in obMASLD (HR 1.735, CI 1.653-1.822). Ethnicity-specific risks included higher myocardial infarction risk in White and Black patients, higher stroke risk in White patients, higher ESRD risk in Black patients, and increased cancers (colorectal, pancreatic and multiple myeloma) among White patients. Incretin-based therapy (IBT) use was markedly lower in nbMASLD but was associated with improved survival. Patients with nbMASLD demonstrated an increased mortality and ethnicity-specific clinical complications than obMASLD despite a more favorable metabolic profile. These findings demonstrate a potential treatment gap for nbMASLD patients and the shortcomings of BMI-based strategies to risk-stratify MASLD patients.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42947552/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42946058
TI  - Thyroid surveillance practices in GLP-1 receptor agonist use and its potential influence on thyroid cancer detection: A scoping review.
AU  - Al-Mashhadani Mustafa
AU  - Khalifa Ahmed
AU  - Al-Mashhadani Yousif
AU  - Ajaz Faika
AU  - Barragan-Leal Camilo
AU  - Alsaeed Maryam
JO  - Diabetes research and clinical practice
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.diabres.2026.113630
AB  - Rodent data prompted a boxed warning for medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC) with glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RAs), yet human findings remain inconsistent and may reflect how intensively patients are screened. We mapped reported thyroid screening practices and detection bias reporting. Following JBI methodology and PRISMA-ScR reporting guidance, we searched PubMed, Scopus, Embase, CENTRAL, and ClinicalTrials.gov from 2010 to July 2026. Empirical studies and trial registry records reporting thyroid screening in adult GLP-1RA users were eligible. Records were charted and synthesised descriptively. Thirty-nine records were included (29 published studies, 10 registry records). Screening was predominantly biochemical. Calcitonin was reported in 16/29 studies (55.2%) and all registry records, whereas ultrasonography was reported in 13/29 (44.8%) and no trials. 19/29 studies (65.5%) described a screening protocol. Screening intensity was reported in 18/29 (62.1%) but detection bias in 11/29 (37.9%). All registry records excluded personal or family history of MTC or multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 before screening. Thyroid screening in GLP-1RA users is heterogeneous, calcitonin-centred, and inconsistently reported, while patients at highest C-cell risk are excluded from trials. Detection bias is seldom addressed despite documented increases in imaging. Standardised reporting of screening intensity is needed to interpret thyroid cancer rates. OSF Protocol Registration Code: 10.17605/OSF.IO/PEBHS.
