ID  - 42938381
TI  - Signature Definition Shapes Associations Between Exenatide-Related Transcriptional Programs and Macrophage Senescence Scores in Colorectal Cancer.
AU  - Song Yunying
AU  - Tayier Aliya
AU  - Peng Yu
AU  - Li Tiankang
AU  - Xu Lijun
AU  - Zhong Minhui
AU  - Yu Chan
AU  - Ma He
JO  - Genes
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.3390/genes17101266
AB  - Background: Treatment-derived expression signatures are often applied to untreated tumor cohorts, although their biological meaning may depend on signature construction and cellular composition. We examined the relationship between an exenatide-derived signature and senescence-associated macrophage expression in colorectal cancer. Methods: Archived pan-cancer and patient-level results were examined alongside a reconstruction from public mouse bulk RNA sequencing and colorectal single-cell matrices. Exenatide signatures were re-derived with tissue and batch adjustment, within-mouse correlation, and one-to-one orthologue mapping. Shared-gene removal, stricter macrophage annotation, expression-matched random signatures, virtual perturbation summaries, and three murine treatment comparisons assessed interpretation and robustness. Results: The original signature was associated with SenMayo in stomach, colon, and rectal tumor analyses. Reconstruction retained 209,207 colorectal cells and 4316 tumor macrophages, with 17 patients eligible for tumor-level analysis. Removing CXCR2 and IL1B from both signatures preserved a positive association (Spearman ρ = 0.804; adjusted β = 1.526; p = 0.00219). The corresponding mean expression association exceeded most expression-matched random signatures (empirical p = 0.006). Associations persisted with a blocked log-expression derivation but were absent with a blocked voom-derived signature using one-to-one orthologues (ρ = 0.056; p = 0.620). Archived virtual IL1B deletion and overexpression predicted opposing shifts after multiplicity adjustment. Treatment-macrophage fold-change correlations remained small (ρ = -0.038 to 0.046), with heterogeneous enrichment. The association was attenuated after adjustment for either inflammatory score-it remained statistically detectable after TNFα/NF-κB adjustment (β = 0.672, 95% CI 0.307-1.037) and was less precisely estimated after broader inflammatory response adjustment (β = 0.383, 95% CI -0.031-0.797). Conclusions: The original exenatide-derived signature was associated with SenMayo scores in colorectal tumor macrophages after removal of shared genes. This association overlapped with inflammatory expression and varied with signature derivation. Dependence on signature derivation limits its interpretation as a pharmacological response marker. These findings support investigation of inflammatory macrophage states without establishing drug-induced senescence.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42938381/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42935806
TI  - Obesity-driven inflammation and immune dysregulation in cancer development and progression.
AU  - Pandey Pritika
AU  - Struder Jeb F
AU  - Rezeli Erika T
AU  - Paules Evan M
AU  - Hursting Stephen D
AU  - Coleman Michael F
AU  - Altwegg Kristin A
JO  - Frontiers in oncology
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.3389/fonc.2026.1947958
AB  - Obesity increases the risk and progression of at least 13 types of cancer. Although the epidemiologic links between excess adiposity and these cancers are well documented, the biological mechanisms linking obesity to carcinogenesis and tumor progression remain incompletely understood. White adipose tissue in obesity is a source of inflammatory and immunosuppressive signals that act systemically, driving metabolic, inflammatory, and immune dysfunction whose consequences differ across tumor types. As adipose tissue expands and adipocytes enlarge, the blood supply becomes inadequate, leading to hypoxia, fibrosis, lipolytic stress, macrophage recruitment, and chronic low-grade inflammation. These obesity-induced perturbations generate endocrine, cytokine, lipid, and metabolic signals that act systemically, within the tumor microenvironment (TME), and/or cell-intrinsically to activate oncogenic and tumor-promoting pathways. The same signals reshape innate and adaptive immunity, promoting tumor-permissive macrophage states and myeloid-derived suppressor cell expansion, while the effector arms weaken as antigen presentation, natural killer cell and T cell function all decline. This review synthesizes current evidence linking obesity-associated inflammation and immune dysregulation to cancer initiation, progression, and treatment responses across multiple obesity-related cancers. Emphasis is placed on adipose tissue inflammation, myeloid cell biology, T cell immunometabolism, endocrine-inflammatory crosstalk, and the relationship between obesity and immunotherapy. Finally, we discuss translational opportunities for preventing or intercepting obesity-related cancers, including weight loss achieved through lifestyle, surgical, or pharmacologic approaches, with an emphasis on GLP-1 receptor agonists given their substantial efficacy, rapid clinical uptake, and potential to reshape obesity-related cancer prevention and survivorship strategies. Defining the metabolic, immune, and inflammatory phenotypes that drive obesity-associated cancer risk and progression will be essential for developing more precise, mechanism-based strategies for prevention, treatment, and survivorship care.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42935806/
ER  - 

