ID  - 42934302
TI  - Glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist use and risk of postmastectomy lymphedema in breast cancer survivors: a multicenter real-world cohort study.
AU  - Yesho Dawit Hailemariam
AU  - Megiso Muluken Zeleke
AU  - Ugwu Chidiebube
AU  - Neely Ariana N
AU  - Verinumbe Tarfa
AU  - Varadi Gabor
AU  - Jones Colton
JO  - Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1007/s00520-026-11405-3
AB  - Postmastectomy lymphedema is a chronic survivorship complication after breast cancer treatment, particularly among patients exposed to axillary surgery. Obesity and metabolic inflammation are recognized contributors to lymphedema risk. This study evaluated whether glucagon-like peptide - 1 receptor agonist exposure was associated with lower incidence of coded postmastectomy lymphedema among breast cancer patients with mastectomy and/or axillary procedure exposure. We performed a retrospective cohort study using deidentified electronic health record data from the TriNetX Research Network. Adult patients with breast cancer and coded mastectomy and/or axillary procedural exposure were stratified by GLP-1 receptor agonist exposure versus no exposure. One-to-one propensity score matching was used to balance measured baseline characteristics. The primary outcome was incident coded postmastectomy lymphedema syndrome. Outcomes were assessed from 1 to 650 days after the index event using risk estimates, Kaplan-Meier analysis, and Cox proportional hazards models. After propensity score matching, 61,630 patients were included, with 30,815 patients in each cohort. Coded postmastectomy lymphedema occurred in 258 of 29,259 GLP-1 receptor agonist users and 779 of 30,237 non-users, corresponding to incidence rates of 0.88% and 2.58%, respectively. GLP-1 receptor agonist exposure was associated with lower incidence of coded postmastectomy lymphedema in time-to-event analysis (hazard ratio 0.373, 95% CI 0.324-0.429; p < 0.001). The absolute risk difference was - 1.7%, with an estimated number needed to treat of 59. In this large real-world breast cancer surgical/procedural cohort, GLP-1 receptor agonist exposure was associated with lower incidence of coded postmastectomy lymphedema over 650 days of follow-up. These findings are hypothesis-generating and require prospective validation with systematic lymphedema assessment and detailed characterization of GLP-1 receptor agonist timing, duration, dose, and adherence.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42934302/
