PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1111/jep.70685
AB  - Clinical medicine, epidemiology, and mechanistic biology each have partial purchase on disease, but none treats the patient (the individual biological system constituted by genetics, biography, environment, and time) as a unifying object of inquiry. Population-level causal inference cannot explain why a specific intervention failed in a specific patient. Cause is the language of populations; mechanism is the language of the individual. To propose a theory of the patient: a scientifically grounded account of how biological, biographical, and environmental processes interacted over time in this person to produce their disease and shape their response to treatment; and to show that it meets the criteria of a scientific theory. Conceptual analysis drawing on Peirce's and Lipton's accounts of abduction, mechanistic philosophy of science (Salmon; Machamer, Darden, and Craver), and the potential outcomes framework, applied to an illustrative case of treatment resistant breast cancer. The theory has three components. Abduction, inference to the best mechanistic explanation of a singular clinical course, is its epistemological foundation. The Mechanistic History Graph (MHG), an individualized, temporal, mechanistic representation whose configurations are competing hypotheses, is its formal structure. Bio-Biographical Mechanistic Profiling (BBMP), which reconstructs the biographical and biological dimensions that clinical science omits, is its empirical content. The MHG for the index case specifies three competing theories of resistance (acquired molecular resistance, biography-driven local aromatase bypass, and CDK4/6 pathway escape) and the evidence that would discriminate among them, testable prospectively by embedding BBMP and pre-specified interaction hypotheses in trials such as pionERA. The framework extends as illustration to darapladib, checkpoint inhibitors, and GLP-1 agonists. A theory of the patient explains, predicts, unifies, generates new observations, and is falsifiable. It complements rather than replaces population-level causal inference, providing a clinical science adequate to the individuality of the patient.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42927628/
